:: StandUpToRival [childhood]
# Finding Your Voice

You take a deep breath and decide to stand up for yourself against your rival's pushing and boasting.

"I was here first," you say firmly, meeting <<if $character.gender eq "female">>her<<else>>his<</if>> eyes. "And we should all take turns fairly."

Your rival seems surprised by your direct challenge. For a moment, <<if $character.gender eq "female">>she<<else>>he<</if>> stares at you, perhaps trying to decide whether to escalate the confrontation or back down.

The other children watch silently, waiting to see what will happen. In the tribe, these childhood conflicts are important - they help establish each person's place in the social order and teach important lessons about standing up for oneself while avoiding dangerous escalation.

After a tense moment, your rival shrugs. "Fine. But I'm still better at this game than you are."

This partial concession allows you both to save face. Your rival has acknowledged your right to your position, while still maintaining a claim to superior skill.

One of the older children, who has been watching, nods approvingly. "Good," she says. "Now we can all play together."

The game continues, and though your rival still tries to show off and dominate, there's a new respect in <<if $character.gender eq "female">>her<<else>>his<</if>> interactions with you. You've shown that you won't be easily pushed aside.

